we're going to begin with a story it  happens to be very close to my life at  the moment in life of millions of others  americans it's an update to a story  we've been following for years now after  the deaths of more than 100 babies in  unregulated infant sleepers the consumer  product safety commission will now begin  regulating the products it's a big deal  it says the move will effectively  eliminate  potential hazards for infants under five  months old  until now there were no safety standards  for infant  sleep products like inclined and in-bed  sleepers  which the advocacy group consumer  reports has linked to  at least 100 infant deaths the sleepers  will face similar regulations as cradles  and bassinets already do  our consumer investigative reporter anna  werner has been reporting on the dangers  of some of these products for years  anna good morning to you so lay it out  for us what does this new rule mean  well tony you know these products are so  popular federal regulators say  roughly one in three families owns  one or more of these products that were  never previously regulated  the new regulations will cover any  inclined sleep  product along with in-bed sleepers and  baby boxes  for infants up to five months old that  covers any product referencing  sleep snooze dream or nap and anything  that depicts sleeping or can be  described as a bed  now millions of these incline products  have been voluntarily recalled  including the fisher-price rock and play  sleeper  nearly 5 million of those were recalled  and the cpsc  linked it to more than 30 deaths in 2019  we spoke to two couples whose infants  died  in a rock and play he was our little  baby our little quota buddy  you're a little cuddle buddy yeah she  was perfect  aria was perfect i don't go a day  without  thinking about her i would like to raise  awareness  there's people out here that are still  using the rock and play sleeper that  our children died in  and guess what despite recalls we went  online this week  and in 10 minutes found more than 50  recalled inclined sleepers  for sale on facebook marketplace  secondhand sleepers now  a facebook spokesperson told us that  those postings violated company policies  prohibiting the sale of recalled goods  and they have been removed a  spokesperson for fisher-price said  product safety is their highest priority  that they voluntarily recalled the rock  and play sleeper more than two years ago  they said they vigilantly monitor  secondary marketplaces  and work hard to remove listings that  they have identified  experts say if you own one of these  products do not use them  stop using them and you should not sale  or sell i'm sorry or donate them to  anybody else  you can reach out to the company for a  refund or a voucher tony yeah and we had  one we actually went back and got that  refund my heart absolutely breaks  for those families something you've  talked about before is  why these products are so potentially  dangerous can you remind us  yeah so one thing people don't realize  you put a baby into an  inclined product where they're slightly  on an angle when they fall asleep their  head can fall forward like this now you  and i we pick our head back up  but these babies they don't have the  muscles to pick their head back  back up so they basically choke off  their airway or  they may turn slightly this way and  because the side of the product is soft  they wind up with their face in the soft  product and they have suffocated  yeah it's such a hard situation because  parents are so desperate for sleep of  any kind and these inclines  can help with digestion and all the rest  but it's important now that the consumer  product safety commission  is going to set some standards so there  is some reassurance that things are safe  anna thank you very much and i do want  to remind viewers that the american  academy of pediatrics says that infants  should sleep on their backs only and  they should also sleep on 